.Dd November 23, 2026
.Dt NETCFG 8
.Os Belka
.Sh NAME
.Nm netcfg
.Nd plumb and configure the IPv4 network
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm plumb
.Nm
.Cm attach
.Ar interface
.Nm
.Cm addr
.Ar interface
.Ar address Ns / Ns Ar length
.Nm
.Cm addr
.Ar interface
.Cm none
.Nm
.Cm route add
.Ar destination
.Ar gateway
.Nm
.Cm route delete
.Ar destination
.Op Ar gateway
.Nm
.Cm forwarding
.Op Cm on | off
.Nm
.Cm show
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Belka's network stack is a set of STREAMS multiplexing drivers:
.Pa /dev/ip
for IPv4, ICMP and ARP, with the interfaces' DLPI devices linked below
it, and
.Pa /dev/udp
and
.Pa /dev/tcp ,
each with an IP stream linked below it.
.Nm
makes those links and configures IP.
The links are persistent: they last after
.Nm
exits.
.Pp
The commands are:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Cm plumb
Link an IP stream under UDP and under TCP, so that
.Dv AF_INET
sockets can be used.
A transport that is plumbed already is left as it is.
.It Cm attach Ar interface
Make an interface of the DLPI device
.Pa /dev/ Ns Ar interface ,
linking it twice under IP, once for IPv4 and once for ARP.
.It Cm addr Ar interface Ar address Ns / Ns Ar length
Give the interface its address, with a subnet mask of
.Ar length
bits, and a route to the subnet.
.It Cm addr Ar interface Cm none
Take the interface's address away, and every route by way of it.
.It Cm route add Ar destination Ar gateway
Add a route to
.Ar destination ,
an
.Ar address Ns / Ns Ar length
or
.Cm default ,
by way of
.Ar gateway ,
which must be reachable on an interface.
The longest prefix that matches a datagram's destination is its route.
.It Cm route delete Ar destination Op Ar gateway
Remove that route.
.It Cm forwarding Op Cm on | off
Forward datagrams for other hosts, as a router does, or stop; or, with
neither, say which.
Forwarding is off until it is turned on.
A forwarded datagram goes on with its time to live one less, or an
ICMP error goes back to its sender: time exceeded, host unreachable,
or fragmentation needed; one that leaves by the interface it came in
on also brings the sender an ICMP redirect.
.It Cm show
List the interfaces, whether IP forwards, the routes and the ARP
cache.
.El
.Pp
Changing the configuration needs the superuser.
The loopback interface,
.Li lo0
(127.0.0.1/8), is always there.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/etc/rc.d/staticroute" -compact
.It Pa /etc/rc.d/network
runs
.Nm
at boot, from the settings in
.Xr rc.conf 5 :
.Va network ,
.Va net_interfaces ,
.Va ifaddr_ Ns Ar interface ,
.Va defaultroute
and
.Va ip_forwarding .
.It Pa /etc/rc.d/staticroute
adds the routes in
.Pa /etc/routed/static_route.conf ,
one a line:
.Ar destination
and
.Ar gateway ,
as
.Cm route add
takes them.
It runs when
.Va staticroute
is set, as it is by default.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
QEMU's user-mode network:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
netcfg plumb
netcfg attach vionet0
netcfg addr vionet0 10.0.2.15/24
netcfg route add default 10.0.2.2
.Ed
